        "text": "The Domestication of the Horse\n\nThe horse was among the last major livestock animals to be brought under human control. Cattle, sheep, and goats were already managed herds in southwest Asia by roughly nine thousand years ago, but the earliest persuasive evidence for horse domestication comes from the grasslands of Central Asia several millennia later. The delay is not surprising. Wild horses were fast, nervous, and capable of injuring a handler, and the open steppe offered them ample room to flee. Only groups with detailed knowledge of horse behavior and a reason to invest in a difficult animal were likely to attempt the process.\n\nEstablishing when that process began has proved unusually difficult. Bones alone are an imperfect guide, since the skeletons of early domestic horses differed little from those of their wild relatives. Archaeologists have therefore turned to indirect indicators. One is the age and sex profile of horse remains at a settlement: a herd kept for milk and meat tends to leave a different pattern of carcasses than a hunted population does. Another is damage to the teeth. A bit held in the mouth can wear the second premolar in a characteristic way, and such wear has been reported at sites in the Eurasian steppe. Residues of mare's milk preserved in pottery provide a third line of evidence.\n\nThe consequences of domestication were far-reaching. A horse converts the low-quality grasses of the steppe into milk, meat, and traction, allowing herders to exploit land that would otherwise support few people. Mounted herders could also manage larger flocks over wider territories. Later, when horses were harnessed to light chariots and then ridden into battle, the military balance across Eurasia shifted repeatedly.\n\nPerhaps the most durable effect was on communication. Distances that had once isolated communities became manageable, and goods, languages, and diseases moved along routes that horses made practical. Whether the animal was domesticated once or several times remains debated, but its influence on Eurasian history is not.",
